          "text": "{\n  \"occurrence_id\": \"bronte-jane-eyre-14913cd0a6a4\",\n  \"label_support\": {\n    \"truth_conditional\": 4,\n    \"performative\": 1,\n    \"exclamatory_reflexive\": 0\n  },\n  \"construals\": [\n    {\n      \"perspective\": \"speaker\",\n      \"analysis\": \"The current speaker quotes Jane's earlier words rather than avowing his own love. He treats the quoted “I love you” as a clear, binding report of Jane's emotional state and invokes it as evidence she cannot retract.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"perspective\": \"hearer\",\n      \"analysis\": \"Jane is positioned as the original speaker whose avowal is being repeated back to her. The current speaker's insistence that she “cannot deny it” and must “repeat it” pressures her to confirm or renew that avowal, but the passage does not provide her response.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"perspective\": \"narrator_or_text\",\n      \"analysis\": \"The text clearly embeds the occurrence within a direct quotation of an earlier utterance and surrounds it with metalinguistic commentary about “words,” articulation, and hearing. At the quoted level it is a truth-conditional avowal; at the present conversational level it functions as cited evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"perspective\": \"reader\",\n      \"analysis\": \"A reader can naturally understand the original words as a sincere assertion of love because they explain Jane's hope of living with Edward. The current speaker's forceful reuse of them may also appear manipulative, although the excerpt alone does not determine Jane's sincerity or intended degree of commitment.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"features\": [\n    \"quoted\",\n    \"metalinguistic\",\n    \"deceptive_or_manipulative\",\n    \"mixed_reading\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"You say you love me, Janet\",\n      \"supports\": \"The current speaker explicitly construes Jane's earlier language as saying that she loves him, strongly supporting a truth-conditional reading.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Those words did not die inarticulate on your lips. I heard them clear and soft\",\n      \"supports\": \"This is metalinguistic attention to the production and audibility of Jane's prior words, confirming that the highlighted occurrence is being quoted and treated as evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"because I love you\",\n      \"supports\": \"The clause supplies a reason for the stated hope of living with Edward, making it propositionally integrated rather than an involuntary exclamation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"the hope of living with you, Edward\",\n      \"supports\": \"The avowal is connected to an imagined shared future, giving weak support to a commitment-related reading, though it does not itself constitute a promise or undertaking.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"you cannot deny it\",\n      \"supports\": \"The current speaker treats the earlier avowal as something that constrains Jane's present position and uses it to foreclose retraction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Do you love me, Jane?—repeat it.\",\n      \"supports\": \"The demand for repetition shows that the quotation is being used to obtain a renewed avowal. It also supports a manipulative or pressuring aspect in the current speaker's deployment, without establishing deception.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"needs_more_context\": true,\n  \"context_request\": \"Jane's original delivery and immediate response to the demand for repetition would help determine sincerity, voluntariness, and whether she understood the avowal as carrying commitment beyond reporting an emotion. Surrounding interaction would also clarify the extent of the current speaker's pressure.\",\n  \"typology_adequate\": false,\n  \"typology_diagnosis\": \"T/P/E describes the force of Jane's quoted original utterance reasonably well: it is predominantly truth-conditional, with at most weak performative commitment and no clear reflexive-exclamatory force. It does not by itself naturally classify the highlighted words at the present discourse level, where another speaker is quoting and metalinguistically invoking them as evidence to compel confirmation.\",\n  \"proposed_missing_dimensions\": [\n    \"original utterance versus present quotative use\",\n    \"illocutionary embedding and revoicing\",\n    \"use of a prior avowal as interpersonal evidence or leverage\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": 0.94\n}"
